Fillets: an analytic blend whose contact passes over a hole or a cut-out of
its face is run on over it and cut by the face beyond (mitcad#121): e.g. a
wall's foot rounded where a hole through the plate in front of the wall
crosses the contact line, or a hole's rim rounded where the hole lies
closer to the part's side than the rounding is wide.

- ChFi3d_Builder::PerformSetOfKPart now first computes each edge's
  analytic blend and splits it into the domains of its faces
  (SplitKPart), keeping a copy of the whole blend, then fills the gaps,
  then builds the stripe from the pieces as before.
- A gap (ChFi3d_FillKPartGaps) is between two consecutive pieces of an
  edge whose contacts on one face N leave it across an edge and come back
  across an edge of the same face beyond B, not tangent to N, while the
  other contacts stay on their face. A piece is inserted there
  (ChFi3d_KPartCutPiece): the blend's surface between the two pieces,
  its other side as before, its side on N replaced by the cut of the blend
  by B (ChFi3d_KPartCutCurve: the arc of the intersection of the two
  surfaces between the points where the contact leaves N and comes back,
  across the blend between its contacts), its points there those of the
  two pieces. Its transition on B is the one on N carried over by the two
  faces' orientations. Where no single arc joins the two points, the gap
  is filled with two pieces cut from each end to its middle
  (ChFi3d_KPartSectionCut).
- ChFi3d_FillKPartGapsAcrossEdges does the same where the contact passes
  over a hole from one edge of the spine to a later one (the edges between
  have no piece of their own).
- The cut's p-curve on the blend is kept between the piece's sections
  (ChFi3d_KeepCutInPiece) where it runs a little behind them (by up to
  ten times the tolerance; the tolerance covers the change).
- A gap is not filled (OCCT's behaviour is kept) where the cut keeps to N's
  surface (the contact only grazes B), where the cut runs past the
  piece's sections (B reaches over the neighbouring pieces too, e.g. a
  hole whose centre lies under the blend), or where a vertex of B lies
  under the blend (an edge of B such as its seam ends on the part of B's
  boundary the blend covers and would have to be prolonged to the cut).
